"# Appendix A — The tiniest possible unit (no module, just verbs)

The other dialect's tiniest module is a six-line `(module manners (export ...) ...)` form. **Motoi has no `module` and no `export`** — both are `unbound` or unparseable. The tiniest reusable unit in Motoi is smaller and plainer: a few top-level `define`s, with a public/private distinction achieved by *nesting*, not by an export list.

```scheme
(define (greet name)    (string-append \"Hello, \"   name \".\"))
(define (farewell name) (string-append \"Goodbye, \" name \".\"))

(list (greet \"Alfred\") (farewell \"Alfred\"))
;; ⇒ (\"Hello, Alfred.\" \"Goodbye, Alfred.\")
```

Two verbs, both public because both are at the top level. There is no module wrapper to open and no export list to maintain — the file *is* the unit.

## Line-by-line

**Line 1** — `(define (greet name) ...)`. A public verb. Any code in the same program can call it.

**Line 2** — `(define (farewell name) ...)`. A second public verb, same status.

That is the whole thing. In the other dialect, line 1 would be `(module manners`, line 2 `(export greet farewell)`, and the verbs would follow. Motoi drops the first two lines entirely: there is nothing to name and nothing to export.

## Adding a private helper

To make a helper *private*, you nest it inside the verb that uses it — lexical scope is Motoi's `export` control (Chapter 9):

```scheme
(define (flip n)
  (define (heads-or-tails k) (if (< k 0.5) 'heads 'tails))  ; PRIVATE
  (heads-or-tails n))

(flip 0.3)
;; ⇒ heads
```

`heads-or-tails` is unreachable from outside `flip` — not because an export list omits it, but because it lives in a scope no outside code can name. That is a stronger guarantee than an export list: it is enforced by how names are looked up.

## What is not here

- **No version declaration.** Motoi has nothing to version — one always-present standard library.
- **No author or signature.** There is no registry to sign for.
- **No description field.** Document with a comment above the `define`.
- **No init side effects to declare.** Top-level code runs top-to-bottom when loaded.

The other dialect's `:version`, `:author`, `:description`, and `:default-lazy` fields on a `module` form have no counterpart, because there is no `module` form.

## What the tiny unit gets you

- Named verbs you can call immediately — no import step.
- Public/private control via nesting (the private helper above).
- The full standard library already in scope (`string-append` here was never imported).

The tiny unit is enough. Everything richer is just more `define`s.

## Try this

Save the two `define`s and the `(list ...)` call to a file. Run it with `./bin/motoi run yourfile.scm` and confirm you get `(\"Hello, Alfred.\" \"Goodbye, Alfred.\")`. Then add the nested `flip`/`heads-or-tails` example and confirm `flip` works while `heads-or-tails` is unreachable from the top level — no `export` list needed, because Motoi has none.

## The reference

For why there is no import, see Chapter 2. For public-vs-private via scope, see Chapters 3 and 9. This appendix gives you the smallest possible Motoi starting point: a couple of `define`s and nothing else, because in a batteries-included Scheme, nothing else is required.")
